rsx: a vertex program reads SET_TRANSFORM_BRANCH_BITS at run time instead of having them baked in.

BRB and CLB branch on one bit of SET_TRANSFORM_BRANCH_BITS (0x1FF8). Since upstream #485 the decompiler baked the
outcome into the program text and the draw engine (and rsx_live_draw) keyed the pipeline on the bits, so every value
of the bits a title set was a program of its own to decompile, translate (glslang + spirv-cross) and compile with
Metal, synchronously on the RSX thread. Resistance 3's effects uber shader is a 543-slot program (57 KB of HLSL,
220 KB of MSL) that picks its optional paths with BRB, and the bits change between draws: cache/msl held 70 variants
of two such programs. One variant measured offline from the cached text: translation ~150 ms, newLibraryWithSource
215-340 ms. In play they came in bursts (9 new programs within 3 s, 7 within one second, 4 of them the uber shader),
which is where the game froze for whole seconds in fights and on entering new spans; a 408 ms frame on landing in the
Haven fight was one RSX drain pass that wrote a new program to cache/msl in the same second. Programs met before cost
under 1.5 ms (the MSL cache and Metal's own library cache both hold across runs), so the stalls were first meetings,
and the bits multiplied those.

The bits now travel with the draw: the decompiler emits
  _pc = (((asuint(vp_posoffset.w) >> N) & 1u) == T) ? target : next;
for BRB (CLB pushes the return slot the same way), and every writer of the VPConst epilogue puts the bits in
vp_posoffset.w (rsx_vp_set_epilogue_branch_bits, lane RSX_VP_BRANCH_BITS_LANE): the draw engine, rsx_live_draw, and
the Metal, D3D12 and Vulkan backends' own paths (which before read whatever bits the last engine decompile had set).
The shader reads only .xyz of vp_posscale and vp_posoffset, and the lane is outside vp_c, so no guest constant or
indexed read reaches it. The programs were already a _pc state machine, so the branch is one more uniform test.
rsx_vp_set_branch_bits and rsx_vp_uses_branch_bits are gone; the bits are no longer pipeline identity.

Tests:
- libs/video/tests/test_metal_vp_branch_bits.m (new, CMake target, macos.yml step): one BRB program through the
  engine and the Metal backend on the GPU draws green with bit 5 set, red with every bit but 5 (0xFFFFFFDF, a NaN as a
  float, reaches the shader as it is) and green again with bits 5 and 31, and the second and third draws compile no
  shader. On the old code the colours are right and the third check fails (a new vertex function per value).
- test_engine_program_identity.c (now run by macos.yml): three draws of one BRB program under different bits build
  one pipeline, and each hands its bits over in the epilogue's last lane; both checks fail on the old code.
- test_shader_msl.c: the BRB/CLB program emits the run-time tests (bit, polarity, targets) and translates to MSL.
- rsx_test_programs.h gains rsx_test_vp_branch (was local to test_shader_msl.c).

Upstream: PR #757.
